# Local harness / roster infrastructure — not part of the published repo.
#
# These are per-workstation roster installs: agent definitions, hooks and skill
# prose. They are regenerated by `/recruit` + the roster installer and carry
# machine-local state, so they are deliberately NOT tracked here.
#
# NOTE — this is the *opposite* of the deterministic review-tool bundle under
# scripts/ + schema/ + tools/data-schema/, which IS tracked (see
# scripts/REVIEW-BUNDLE.md). Those files are the quality gates themselves; a
# fresh clone must get them or the project has no gates at all. Do not add
# bundle paths here. `scripts/check-review-bundle-tracked.sh` enforces that.
.agents/
.harness/
docs/team-proposal-2026-05-29.md

# `kb/` USED TO BE LISTED ABOVE AND IS NOW TRACKED (backlog-100).
#
# It is not roster install state. It is ~4200 lines of code-anchored project
# knowledge — per-area notes verified against named commits, with open questions
# and retracted claims recorded in place — plus `kb/properties.md`, whose fenced
# `code-intel` block `scripts/check-kb-properties.sh` executes in CI. Untracked,
# a fresh clone got none of it and the block would have been a set of invariants
# nothing runs.
#
# `skills-meta/` STAYS IGNORED, deliberately, and this is not an oversight.
# `skills-meta/friction.jsonl` is session telemetry, not project knowledge: it
# quotes the operator's own messages verbatim, in French, with transcript line
# numbers, alongside in-the-moment self-assessments written to be blunt rather
# than published. None of it tells a contributor anything about SPOC or Sarek.
# The durable part — the class vocabulary, and the `gate-vacuous` catalogue that
# is the most expensive thing this campaign learned — has been lifted into
# `kb/glossary.md` and `kb/properties.md`, where it is tracked and, in the
# properties' case, executed. The telemetry it was extracted from stays local.
skills-meta/

# Roster runtime install directories (machine-local, installed by /recruit).
/roster/
/skills/
